<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Opoku Ware I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1720–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Opoku Ware I was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>, or ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, from about 1720 to 1750, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>. His campaigns greatly expanded the empire's territory. Accounts of the Ashanti Empire note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> observers reported Asante armies of 50,000 men in the field during his campaigns.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Opoku Ware, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyoko-clan">Oyoko</a> heritage, came to the throne during civil disorder after the death of Osei Kofi Tutu I, which scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivor-wilks">Ivor Wilks</a>, John Kofi Fynn and Albert Adu Boahen place around 1712 or 1717. He established his power in 1720–1721. His first task was to put down a revolt of the Akyem, Wassa, Aowin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a>, beginning with an attack on Akyem in 1720–21. The Aowin king Ebrimoro had earlier sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> and carried off or killed members of the royal family, Opoku Ware's mother among them; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> defeated the Aowin and annexed their territory of Ahafo.</p><p>Conquest followed for two decades. Bono was absorbed in 1723–24 and Wassa invaded in 1726, forcing its king Ntsiful I to move his capital to Abrade near the coast. Western Gonja and Gyaman were invaded in 1732 and Banda in 1740. In 1742 the Akyem states of Abuakwa and Kotoku were incorporated and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> occupied; the Ga accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> overrule but won an exemption from tribute, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> paid a regular stipend as rent for their fort at Accra. Eastern Gonja was absorbed by 1744 and Dagbon conquered in 1744–1745.</p><p>At home Opoku Ware turned the Gyase, until then charged with the palace's domestic duties, into an arm of state administration, adding the Gyasewa, Dadiesoaba and Ananta. He created the Nsumankwahene as the king's physician, set over the medicine men and priests, and, according to Reindorf, took up death duties and standard gold weights on the advice of Amo Yaw, the conquered king of Techiman. The Danish agent Nog saw his cloth workshops weave unravelled imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> into local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his later years Opoku Ware proposed laws to curb the provincial chiefs, who had grown powerful with the empire's expansion. According to Joseph Dupuis, writing in the 1820s, a conspiracy formed in the capital and the king had to flee his palace by night to Juaben. There he gathered allied chiefs, defeated the rebels and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, pardoning them on condition that his policy be respected. He died in 1750 before he could carry it out and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusi-obodom">Kusi Obodom</a>. The historian Hagan argues that his attempt to reduce the aristocracy's power inspired the bureaucratic reforms of Asantehene Osei Kwadwo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Opoku Ware I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>European observers reported Asante armies of tens of thousands in the field during the campaigns of <b>Opoku Ware I</b> (r. c. 1720–1750), who conquered Bono, Gyaman and the southern parts of Gonja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1723 the Ashanti Empire under <b>Opoku Ware</b> conquered Bono Manso and destroyed the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/bono-state">Bono State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.7,-1.63&amp;year=1739" aria-label="Kumasi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4505 1913 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Opoku Ware I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4955 2183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4955 2183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.7,-1.63&amp;year=1739">What was here in 1739? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
